EX-J15 Full-Resolution Chroma DC Quantile Mapping — HONEST-STOP (2026-05-28)
============================================================================

Status: [RULED OUT — opt-in env hook + helper SHIPPED for future A/B, default OFF]

EX-J15 was queued in docs/JXL_ENCODER_LEARNINGS.md as:
  > separate ANS context models per Y/Cb/Cr AND per frequency band tier
  > (LF/MF/HF). Currently single shared per-channel. Spec ALLOWS multiple
  > per-symbol contexts (this is what `context_map` is for), so this is
  > wire-compatible

Per the dispatch task, the highest-EV interpretation that's
wire-format-compatible is to give EACH chroma channel its OWN block
context per luma DC bucket (instead of libjxl's half-resolution
n + i/2 collapse). This is the closest analog to Minnen 2020's
"channel-wise autoregressive" idea that fits inside the JXL spec's
hard wire-format bounds (num_ctxs <= 16, num_dc_ctxs * (qft+1) <= 64).

Mechanism shipped (default-OFF):
- New `BlockCtxMap::jpeg_dc_quantile_ex_j15()` in
  `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/ac_context.rs` — same DC quantile thresholds
  as the libjxl-parity `jpeg_dc_quantile()`, but emits a ctx_map that
  gives Y={0..n-1}, Cb={n..2n-1}, Cr={2n..3n-1} (full-resolution
  chroma) when num_dc_ctxs <= 5 (3*n <= 16). Falls back to libjxl
  half-resolution mapping for n > 5 (mandatory: 3*6 = 18 > 16, spec
  failure) and for grayscale (nothing to expand).
- Wiring in `jxl-encoder/src/jpeg/encode.rs` — env-gated by
  `EX_J15_FULL_CHROMA={1,true,on,yes}`. Treats "0"/"false"/"off"/"no"
  and unset as OFF (so the bench can pass `=0` for the baseline arm).
- 3 unit tests (jpeg_dc_quantile_ex_j15_full_chroma,
  jpeg_dc_quantile_ex_j15_grayscale_falls_back,
  jpeg_dc_quantile_ex_j15_large_n_falls_back).

=== HARD WIRE-FORMAT CONSTRAINTS THAT BLOCK THE BIGGER GAIN ===

The dispatch's "Option A" (block-level nzeros tier) and "Option B"
(per-source-SOS band) are STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE per libjxl
`ac_context.h:101-110` decoder formula:

  block_ctx = ctx_map[c_swapped * NUM_ORDERS + ord]
                     * (qf_thresholds.size() + 1) + qf_idx]
                     * num_dc_ctxs + dc_idx]

There is no fourth axis. The decoder cannot compute "this block's
nzeros tier" before reading the block's tokens (which `block_ctx`
selects). Adding a freq-band axis on top of `block_ctx` is a
wire-format change.

What IS available wire-format-safe is:
(a) Expand `dc_thresholds[0]` (X channel) and `dc_thresholds[2]`
    (B channel) to give chroma their own DC quantile buckets.
(b) Change the `ctx_map` permutation to give chroma full-resolution
    contexts instead of libjxl's half-resolution collapse.

(a) doesn't help for JPEG re-encode at low bitrates because chroma DC
dynamic range is small and most chroma blocks have nzeros=0 (the AC
contexts are starved for training data, not over-clustered).

(b) is what we shipped above, and the measurement is conclusive that
it REGRESSES on this corpus.

=== MEASUREMENT ===

Sample: 200 mixed baseline + progressive JPEGs from
  /home/lilith/product-images + /home/lilith/work/codec-corpus,
  random.seed(11), stratified ~33%/67% baseline/progressive.
  Same seed and file pool as the W44-216 dispatch baseline cited in
  the dispatch task ("currently +1.19% above cjxl").

Encoders:
- cjxl: libjxl v0.12.0 (--lossless_jpeg=1 -e 7)
- ours-base: cjxl-rs at this branch HEAD (--lossless-jpeg -e 7) with
  EX_J15_FULL_CHROMA UNSET in env.
- ours-exj15: cjxl-rs at this branch HEAD (--lossless-jpeg -e 7) with
  EX_J15_FULL_CHROMA=1 in env.

Roundtrip: djxl --reconstruct_jpeg, byte-identical compare against
the source JPEG file. Both arms roundtrip OK on every file.

Results:
  Files attempted:     200
  Encode failures:     0
  Roundtrip diffs:     0    (both arms)
  Source total:        21,375,714 bytes
  cjxl total:          17,096,177 bytes
  ours-base total:     17,325,302 bytes  (+1.340 % vs cjxl)
  ours-exj15 total:    17,334,059 bytes  (+1.391 % vs cjxl)

  EX-J15 vs base:      +0.0505 % (+8,757 bytes)
  Per-file: wins=71  losses=129  ties=0
  Loss/win bytes ratio: 3.28x

Per-size-bucket breakdown (from the TSV):
  <50KB:        n=1   wins=1  losses=0  total_delta=-18 bytes
  50-100KB:     n=80  wins=30 losses=50 total_delta=+3,138 bytes
  100-200KB:    n=117 wins=40 losses=77 total_delta=+5,479 bytes
  200-500KB:    n=2   wins=0  losses=2  total_delta=+158 bytes

Losses dominate in EVERY size bucket. No file-size discriminator
can rescue this — the structural cost is the same across all sizes.

=== ROOT CAUSE (POST-HOC) ===

The clustering input grows from 3,960 input contexts
(= 8 block_ctxs × 495 ZD slots) to up to 5,940 (= 12 × 495) when
EX-J15 is on. The output cluster count grows by ~30 % too.

Each new cluster carries an ANS distribution-header cost of
~50-150 bits (depending on the alphabet width — 8 to 30 active
symbols per cluster on JPEG AC streams). With ~30 extra clusters
× ~80 bits = ~300 extra bytes per image of header overhead.

This is consistent with the observed average regression of
+44 bytes/image: most files DO get marginally better data compression
from the extra context separation, but the header overhead exceeds
the data savings on the majority of the corpus. On the 71 wins, the
data savings happen to clear the header threshold (mostly larger
files where header is amortized over more tokens).

A win-class would require either:
(a) Eliminating the header cost (impossible — ANS requires per-cluster
    distributions).
(b) Substantially larger data savings (would require a STRUCTURAL
    context model improvement, not a per-channel split — the per-Y/Cb/Cr
    distributions just aren't different enough at low bitrate to
    overcome a 0.5-1 bit-per-token header cost amortized over the
    ~80,000 average per-file token count).

=== DO NOT (BINDING FOR FUTURE AGENTS) ===

1. DO NOT default-flip `EX_J15_FULL_CHROMA=1`. Measurement is
   conclusive across 200 files (loss/win bytes ratio 3.28x).
2. DO NOT respawn an EX-J15 variant that adds MORE block contexts
   (e.g. via populating dc_thresholds[0] and dc_thresholds[2]). Same
   structural issue: more clusters → more headers → cost > savings on
   typical JPEG AC streams at q≤50.
3. DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for the +0.0505 % regression (per
   W44-66). The bytes change because the cluster count and context
   map size physically grow.
4. DO NOT remove the `jpeg_dc_quantile_ex_j15` helper or the env
   hook. They are the regression harness for any future investigator
   wanting to A/B different chroma DC mappings against the libjxl
   half-resolution baseline.
5. DO NOT respawn EX-J15 expecting the "2-4 % bpp at low bitrates"
   number from JXL_ENCODER_LEARNINGS.md. That gain is achievable in
   THEORY for the Minnen 2020-style autoregressive setup but is
   structurally blocked by the JXL wire format's 16-block-ctx cap and
   ANS's per-cluster header cost. The doc should be updated to
   reflect this.

=== FOLLOW-ON CANDIDATES (NOT IN EX-J15 SCOPE) ===

A. Audit `compute_huffman_data_cost` / `histogram_distance` to
   verify the pair-merge cost model correctly weights the ANS
   distribution-header cost when admitting/rejecting splits. If the
   pair-merge optimizer is over-eager (under-counting header cost),
   then EVEN THE DEFAULT 8-ctx mapping may have too many clusters,
   and reducing input contexts (going to a FLAT compact map) might
   save bytes. (Reverse-direction lever.)

B. Per-image discriminator at the WIRE LEVEL: detect when source JPEG
   has unusually-skewed chroma DC distribution (e.g. via Hasler-Süsstrunk
   colorfulness ≤ 30 or similar zenanalyze-like proxy) and ONLY then
   fire EX-J15. The 71 wins concentrate in files where chroma DC
   distribution is multi-modal (heuristically — would need proper
   measurement to confirm).

C. Pre-LZ77 token re-ordering to improve the existing 8-ctx histogram
   clustering (mentioned as alternative entropy-side lever above).

=== FILES ===

- jxl-encoder/src/vardct/ac_context.rs — new
  `BlockCtxMap::jpeg_dc_quantile_ex_j15()` + 3 unit tests.
- jxl-encoder/src/jpeg/encode.rs — env-gated dispatch at the existing
  `BlockCtxMap::jpeg_dc_quantile` call site.
- benchmarks/jpeg_exj15_freqband_2026-05-28.tsv — per-file bytes.
- benchmarks/jpeg_exj15_freqband_2026-05-28.meta — this file.
